WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770 position:56%
China is home to 1.4 billion people.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:08.770 position:61%
That means about 1 out of every 5
people around the world lives in China.

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:13.480
But the country is facing
a population crisis.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.348
The Chinese population shrinking

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.249
for the first time

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.385
since the Great Famine in 1961.

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:22.422 position:59%
China's population could shrink by 48%

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:25.258 position:61%
between now and the end of the century.

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:26.793
China's population decline

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:29.362 position:64%
has really come faster than was expected.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.364
It could mean economic stagnation.

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:32.365
It could also mean that

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:37.203
China may never eclipse the US
as the world's largest economy.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.978
To maintain its current population,

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:46.780 position:56%
every woman in China must give birth

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.349
to two children in her lifetime.

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:50.650
But China's fertility rate

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.753 position:59%
has dropped well below that threshold.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.121
Urban households have

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:56.856
even lower fertility rates.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:01:01.528 position:66%
Shanghai's fertility rate in 2022 was 0.7.

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:03.430
That's lower than South Korea,

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.033
the country with the world's
lowest fertility rate.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.001
In past decades,

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.736 position:61%
China has really been dependent on this

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:13.306 position:61%
vast labor force for the economic boom.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:15.375
As this labor force is shrinking,

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:18.311
will China still have
the same driving force

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:22.415
for its economic growth but also
for global economic growth?

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:26.686
China's working-age population
already started to contract

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:30.290
around 2015 or 2016.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.292
In the next three decades,

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:34.260 position:58%
In the next three decades until 2050,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.895
the working-age population

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:39.199
can further drop by 300 million.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:40.300
In the longer term,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.502
we have fewer people working

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.538 position:64%
but supporting more people in retirement.

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.974
Less demand, less output,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:51.077 position:64%
we're just entering such a vicious cycle.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.480
We had the government
think tank estimating that

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.149 position:61%
the main pension fund for urban workers

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.852
will hit zero by 2035.

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:01.554
It could leave huge burdens
for a lot of elderly people

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.056
and their offspring

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:06.126
who are not taken care of by
the social security system.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.229
Beijing really needs to not only
shift its growth model,

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.631 position:58%
but also it needs to rapidly increase

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.733 position:58%
spending on pensions and health care.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.669
If it fails, it could
mean economic stagnation.

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:18.138
And it could also mean that China

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:22.442
may never eclipse the US
as the world's largest economy.

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:26.579
China's GDP growth can
slow from 5% at present

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.815
to like 2% in 30 years.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.651
So for those who are
relying on China's demand,

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:35.188
they have to prepare for
a slowing China economy.

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.589
This also means opportunity

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:39.125 position:61%
for some emerging economies like India,

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:41.861 position:59%
who also want to grow at a fast speed,

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:45.064
trying to gain more share
in the global economy.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:47.367
They still have quite
strong population growth,

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:52.005
which can be an important
driver for their GDP growth.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:55.975
But for a long time,

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:59.712
China wanted to keep its
population growth under control.

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:03.249
As a shortage of resources put
pressure on the developing country,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:06.186 position:66%
the government introduced a policy in 1980

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:09.422
that limits families
to have just one child.

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:12.192
The government implemented
the policy through fines.

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:13.560
There's potential loss of jobs

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.328
if you are a government employee.

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:19.365
But also in many cases, forced
abortions and sterilizations.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:23.703
There is a saying in
traditional Chinese culture

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.705 position:63%
that explains how people view fertility.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.341 position:58%
That is: the most disrespectful thing

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:30.310
someone can do to their family
is to not have children.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.946
But that doesn't include daughters.

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:36.149 position:66%
Because only sons can inherit family names

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:38.685
and family wealth.

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:42.522
This led to China's boy-preference.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:46.226
As parents were allowed
to have fewer babies,

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:49.529
some people resorted to illegal
means to determine a baby's gender.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:51.998
This led to more abortions when

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:55.802
parents determined their
child would be a girl.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.371 position:61%
The normal range for sex ratio at birth

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:02.475 position:61%
The normal range for sex ratio at birth

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.510
Since the 1980s,

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.981
this ratio in China has
stayed well above that.

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:09.916 position:56%
The most recent census suggests that

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:12.585
there are over 32 million more men

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.188
than women in China.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:17.290
With a lack of female population

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.459 position:61%
and decades of stringent birth control,

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.628
the government had reduced
the number of births

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:23.162
in the last few decades by

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.065
But now,

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:29.068
they are facing a tough
challenge of reversing that.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:31.304
These demographic
challenges have attracted

00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:33.239
attention from the highest levels,

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:34.907
with President Xi saying that

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:38.211
China is going to focus
on boosting birthrates

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:41.914
and also take a proactive response
to the aging population.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:45.718
So, the government has really
reversed this one-child
policy at a very rapid clip.

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:50.123 position:63%
In 2016, it officially ended that policy
in place of the two-child policy.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:51.591
And even two years ago,

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:53.860
when we had then again
the three-child policy,

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.461
this has really done very little

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.096
to put a halt to this

00:04:57.096 --> 00:05:00.133 position:58%
very rapidly dropping fertility rate.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:04.170
Having babies looks
like a personal choice.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.639 position:66%
But parents all have to take the country's

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:11.639
social security system and
benefits into consideration.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:15.715
I recently saw a blog
post from a Chinese woman.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:18.451
She says her monthly income
is ¥6,000 CNY ($872 USD)

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.353
If she's raising a child

00:05:20.353 --> 00:05:25.353
it takes most of her salary to
cover her child's everyday needs.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:27.460
Some parents are complaining that

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.997
the cost to send their
kids to kindergarten is as
expensive as college tuition.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.832
Even if both parents are working,

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:36.869
they have to dedicate all of
one person's salary to childcare.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:40.640
That's a huge burden
on any ordinary family.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.043
And that's just daily expenses.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:46.612 position:63%
China's homes are notoriously expensive.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:50.650 position:58%
It's almost impossible for couples to
depend on their income to pay for it.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:53.453
They either have to get
help from their parents

00:05:53.453 --> 00:05:55.655
or resort to other
sources to get that money.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:58.057 position:58%
Some local governments reward parents

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:00.727 position:66%
with a few thousand CNY for having a baby.

00:06:00.727 --> 00:06:03.896
But this makes little
difference for couples.

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:08.034
Because having a child
is a lifelong commitment.

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:10.002
Because having a child is
a life-long commitment.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.839
China's government, especially
in the very top echelons,

00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:14.741
are very much male dominated.

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:17.343
One criticism of some of
these policies is that

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:22.315
they just fall so woefully short
and they are very male-centric.

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:24.384
They're more superficial responses

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:27.220 position:61%
to very deeply entrenched social issues

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.255
that women are demanding change.

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:32.825
We see more and more women hoping

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:37.697
to get more involved in making
and changing laws and regulations.

00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:40.366
But unfortunately,

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:45.104
Chinese media outlets
are heavily censored.

00:06:45.104 --> 00:06:49.809
There's not enough coverage
and discussion on this topic.

00:06:49.809 --> 00:06:51.778
To ensure that economic growth

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:54.380 position:61%
doesn't slow with this population drag,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.382 position:59%
Beijing has some tools in its toolkit.

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:58.251
It can raise retirement age.

00:06:58.251 --> 00:06:59.552
It can also encourage people

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:01.854
to move from the rural
areas into the cities.

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:04.390
And it is already investing heavily

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:07.026
in vocational education as well as

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:10.530 position:64%
in automation and science and technology.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.966 position:58%
Population decline is a global issue.

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:16.402
We're all preparing for a world

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:19.605 position:63%
with fewer population and fewer workers.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:22.442 position:58%
China probably has slowed much faster

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:25.144 position:63%
than other countries at a similar stage.

00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:26.779
But I think other economies,

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.081
they are also seeing a
slowdown in their growth.

00:07:29.081 --> 00:07:30.283
In the longer run,

00:07:30.283 --> 00:07:32.518
it's not necessarily that

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:34.320
when China slows

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.089
other economies will gain
from China's decline.

